course: dictionary
lesson: 12616
title: Losing respect: disgrace
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Disgrace means losing other people's respect because you did something very bad or shameful. «Sam broke the window and then lied about it, so he fell into disgrace with his teacher.» The clues are broke, lied and fell: the teacher no longer thinks well of him.
explain: A disgrace can also be a thing or a person that is so bad it is shocking. «This mess is a disgrace!» The speaker is angry, and means the mess should not be like this. Adults often say this when they are cross about something messy or unfair.
explain: To disgrace someone means to bring shame on them, like your family or your school. «Mia did not disgrace her team; she owned up and played fair.» Ask: what word would fit here? Shame or loss of respect fits, so disgrace is the right idea. Try your meaning in the sentence to check it.
fact: Disgrace means losing other people's respect because of something bad or shameful you did.
fact: «In disgrace» means you are in trouble and people think less of you.
fact: «A public disgrace» means something shameful that everyone can see.
fact: «Bring disgrace on» means to make other people, such as your family, feel ashamed.
fact: After a disgrace you can still say sorry and rebuild trust, but it takes time.
exercise: «Tom was sent to his room in disgrace after he lied to his mum.» What does in disgrace mean? | Happy and proud of his work | Ready for a party | Hungry and tired | *Losing people's respect because of something bad
exercise: «This muddy, rubbish-filled park is a disgrace!» What is the speaker saying? | The park has a brand new swing | *The park is shocking and should not be like this | The park is the best place in town | The park is closed for the day
exercise: Choose the word that means to bring shame on someone. | to tickle | *to disgrace | to praise | to forgive
test: «After the cheating, the player fell into disgrace and lost his place in the team.» What does fell into disgrace mean? | Went on holiday | Became very tall | Was given a prize | *Lost people's respect after doing wrong
test: Sara's lie was not only trouble for her; it was a disgrace to her whole class. What does disgrace mean here? | A happy surprise | A new game | *Something shameful that brought shame on the class | Being very tired
exam: Which sentence shows someone who can rebuild trust after a disgrace? | Ben blamed his brother for it | Ben hid the broken vase under his bed | *Ben said sorry, owned up to his mistake and helped clean up | Ben laughed and walked off
